=== Mannenbashi Bridge ===
[[File:Sasamuta-jinja Mannen-bashi.JPG|thumb|right|Mannen Bridge (prefectural designated cultural property)]]
``Mannenbashi'' (Mannenbashi) is a stone single-arch bridge that spans the {{ill|Ōita-gawa|Q11432686|qid=Q11432686}} water system Samuta River (commonly known as the Misogi River), which flows through the entrance of Nishikanta Shrine. It was designated as a {{ill|Tangible Cultural Property (Japan)|Q2901860|qid=Q2901860}} by Oita Prefecture on April 8, 1955 ({{ill|1980|Q2439|qid=Q2439}}).

Not only is the main structure an arch, but the road surface also forms a gentle arc, and the stonework between the road surface and the arch is narrow. At the end of the {{ill|Edo period|Q184963|qid=Q184963}}, in {{ill|Bunkyū|Q730266|qid=Q730266}}2 ({{ill|1862|Q7698|qid=Q7698}}), jalink|Nobeoka Domain|Q10863441}} was founded by the village headman of Samuta Village, which was the territory of {{ill|Oka Domain|Q8192275|qid=Q8192275}}, {{ill|Ōno District, Ōita|Q8081661|qid=Q8081661|lt=Ono District}} It was completed in the same year by 20 people, including Gobei Goto, the second generation stone mason from Shibakita Village (currently {{ill|Bungo-Ōno|Q850366|qid=Q850366}} Shibakita, Inukai Town).

'''Specifications'''
* Location: Samuta, Oita City, Oita Prefecture
* River: Oita River system Samuda River
* Format: Stone single arch bridge
* Bridge length: 22.0 meters
* Bridge width: 3.7 meters
*Span: 11 meters
* Katsuya (arch height): 3.9 meters
* Completion: Bunkyu 2 (1862)* Stonemason: Gobei Goto (2nd generation)
* Cultural property designation, etc.: Oita Prefecture designated tangible cultural property

